Car Rental Management System

Objective
You will build a console-based Car Rental Management System in Java. The
program must allow a rental office employee to register vehicles, register
customers, rent and return cars, and view reports — all through a text menu. The
entire program must be written using only the topics listed below.

Scenario
SpeedWay Rentals is a small car rental office located near the airport. For the last
eight years the office has managed everything on paper: a thick notebook for the
cars, a second notebook for customers, and a stack of handwritten receipts held
together by a rubber band.
The system worked — until last summer. During the holiday rush, the same car was
promised to two different customers on the same morning. One of them had already
paid. To make things worse, when the manager tried to calculate the monthly
income, he had to add up 340 handwritten receipts by hand, and the total was
wrong twice.
The owner, Mrs. Nadia, has decided that this will not happen again. She has hired
you to build a simple console application that her employees can use at the front
desk. She does not need a beautiful interface or a database — she needs something
reliable that runs on the office computer, keeps track of which cars are available,
remembers who rented what, and calculates the money correctly every single time.
She has two types of vehicles in her fleet: regular cars rented per day, and luxury
cars which are also rented per day but carry an extra insurance fee and require a
minimum rental period. Both share most of the same information, so she expects
your design to avoid repeating yourself.

Functional Requirements
The application must allow the employee to:
1. Add a new regular car to the fleet.
2. Add a new luxury car to the fleet.

3. Add a new customer.
4. Display all cars in the fleet.
5. Display only the currently available cars.
6. Rent a car to a customer for a number of days.
7. Return a rented car.
8. Search for a car by its ID.
9. Search for a car by brand name.
10. Display all customers together with the car each one currently holds.
11. Display statistics for the office (total income, number of rented cars, most
expensive car, average daily price).
12. Exit the program safely.

Car
Purpose
Represents a single vehicle in the fleet. This is the parent class for all vehicle types.
It stores the vehicle’s identity, its price, and whether it is currently rented.
Fields
Each car has id, brand, model, year, price per day, available or not, car
count and tax rate
Special Requirements
• All fields must be private. The outside world may only reach them through
methods.
• There must be no setter for id, brand, model, or year — once a car is
registered these details cannot change.
• Car count represents total number of cars.
• Tax rate will always be 0.14
• this must be used inside the constructors to separate parameters from
fields, and inside this(...) for constructor chaining.

Luxury Car
Purpose
Represents a premium vehicle. A luxury car is a car, but it carries an additional
insurance fee and cannot be rented for fewer than a fixed minimum number of days.
Fields
Each luxury car has insurance fee and minimum number of rental days

• insurance fee — a fixed extra amount added once to every rental of this car.
• Minimum number of rental days — the smallest number of days a luxury
car may be rented for. Which will be 3 days.

Customer
Purpose
Represents a person who rents cars from the office and keeps track of which car
that person currently holds.
Fields
Each customer has id, name, phone, rented car id, number of rented days
, total paid and count
Special Requirements
• All fields private; access through methods only.
• Count represent total number of customers.
• Rented car id represents id for the rented car, or -1 if the customer has no
car.
• Total paid — the sum of everything this customer has ever paid to the
office.
• Number of rented days — how many days the current rental lasts (0 when
nothing is rented).

Main Class
The Main class contains the main method and controls the whole program. It must
not contain business calculations that belong inside Car, LuxuryCar, or Customer —
Main only reads input, calls methods, and prints results.
Required constants
Define 2 numbers:
- Max number of cars which will be 20.
- Max number of customers which will be 20.
Program flow
1. Print a welcome banner.
2. Display the menu.
3. Read the employee’s choice.
4. Execute the chosen operation.
5. On exit, print a goodbye message together with the total number of cars, the
total number of customers, and the total income.

